%description:

Tcp.checksumMode:invalid -- a value that is not in the @enum stops the run with an error.

The claim (Tcp.ned): "Checksums are declared correct rather than computed, unless
`checksumMode` is "computed" (MUST-2 asks for computed checksums; gap 2 of the TCP
evidence)".

The classes: declared ("declared", the default: the sender writes no checksum and marks the
header as correct) and computed ("computed": the sender computes the checksum of RFC 9293).
The code: Tcp::initialize() puts a TcpChecksumInsertionHook into the IPv4 and IPv6 modules
of the host for "computed"; Tcp::checkChecksum() of the receiver follows the mode that the
header carries, so the parameter of the sender decides whether a receiver can find a wrong
checksum. An invalid value fails at the @enum of the NED. This test separates "invalid" from the two names.

Observation: level 0, the run stops at the start with the error of the @enum.

%# parameter: Tcp.checksumMode
%# class: invalid
%# value: "none"
%# key: *.host1.tcp.checksumMode
%# expected-result: PASS

%activity:
EV << "TcpChecksumModeInvalid compiled\n";

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
*.host1.tcp.checksumMode = "none"    # the parameter under test
include ../../parameter/ini/_bulk.ini
include ../../parameter/ini/_base.ini

%exitcode: 1

%contains-regex: stderr
Value "none" is not allowed, must be one of: "declared","computed"
